    "narrative": "Our core strategy today centers on capitalizing on the Roki Sasaki matchup, which yields the highest ceiling plays of the slate. **Luis Arraez** (69.53) and **Jung Hoo Lee** (68.02) serve as our primary engines; both benefit significantly from the elevated SP_xERA of 5.30, providing a high-floor opportunity to pad our AVG and R categories. Similarly, **Taylor Ward** (68.49) is a standout play, bolstered by a 16% park factor boost and a favorable platoon dynamic against the lefty Ryan Weathers, pushing him well into our \"Strong\" tier. **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (69.50) rounds out the top-tier options, maintaining elite efficiency despite a moderate matchup, ensuring we keep the RBI production consistent.\n\nThe bench decision process was tightest around **Daulton Varsho** (56.24). While he narrowly missed the starting cut due to the density of higher-scoring options in our OF slots, his matchup against Drew Rasmussen is compelling. **Varsho** offers a critical \"Slow SP Delivery\" bonus, which is vital given our mandate to hunt stolen bases; he remains our primary pivot if any of the \"Lineup Pending\" statuses for **Will Smith** or **Geraldo Perdomo** result in late scratches. We are closely monitoring **Will Smith** (47.63), whose projection took a sharp 19% hit due to the elite 2.60 xERA of Trevor McDonald. While **Smith** is a marginal start, his stability at C1 keeps him in the lineup, though he is the most susceptible to being benched if we require a defensive replacement late in the day.\n\nRisks remain centered on the \"Lineup Pending\" designations across the board. We are currently banking on **Brendan Donovan** (65.19) maintaining the leadoff spot to capture a 10% order bonus; should he drop down the order, his efficiency score will recalibrate downward. Additionally, while **Jeremiah Jackson** (49.98) offers enticing SB potential against the slow delivery of Ryan Weathers, his #9 spot in the lineup limits his overall utility today. We 